1
00:00:01,381 --> 00:00:02,682
(Q21(table)) Hello, Helen.

2
00:00:02,682 --> 00:00:03,542
Sorry I'm late.

3
00:00:03,542 --> 00:00:05,276
Hi, Jeremy, no problem.

4
00:00:05,276 --> 00:00:09,086
Well we'd better work out where we are on our project, I suppose.

5
00:00:09,086 --> 00:00:10,137
Yeah.

6
00:00:10,137 --> 00:00:18,675
I've looked at the drawings you've done for my story, 'The Forest', and I think they're brilliant - they really create the atmosphere I had in mind when I was writing it.

7
00:00:18,675 --> 00:00:20,248
I'm glad you like them.

8
00:00:20,248 --> 00:00:23,842
There are just a few suggestions I'd like to make.

9
00:00:23,842 --> 00:00:24,323
Go ahead.

10
00:00:24,323 --> 00:00:35,179
[洞穴插图] Now, I'm not sure about the drawing of the cave - it's got trees all around it, which is great, but the drawing a bit too static, isn' it?

11
00:00:35,179 --> 00:00:37,279
I think it needs some action.

12
00:00:37,279 --> 00:00:39,323
Yes, there's nothing happening.

13
00:00:39,323 --> 00:00:43,771
Perhaps I should add the boy - Malcolm, isn't it?

14
00:00:43,771 --> 00:00:45,699
He would be walking up to it.

15
00:00:45,699 --> 00:00:49,017
Yes, let's have Malcolm in the drawing.

16
00:00:49,017 --> 00:00:54,712
And what about putting in a tiger - the one that he makes friends with a bit later?

17
00:00:54,712 --> 00:00:58,833
Maybe it could be sitting under a tree washing itself.

18
00:00:58,833 --> 00:01:03,686
And the tiger stops in the middle of what it's doing when it sees Malcolm walking past.

19
00:01:03,686 --> 00:01:04,805
That's a good idea.

20
00:01:04,805 --> 00:01:06,472
OK, I'll have a go at that.

21
00:01:06,472 --> 00:01:10,437
[狂欢插图] Then there's the drawing of the crowd of men and women dancing.

22
00:01:10,437 --> 00:01:14,314
They've just outside the forest, and there's a lot going on.

23
00:01:14,314 --> 00:01:20,521
That's right, you wanted them to be watching a carnival procession, but I thought it would be too crowded.

24
00:01:20,521 --> 00:01:22,316
Do you think it works like this?

25
00:01:22,316 --> 00:01:25,600
Yes, I like what you've done.

26
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:31,803
The only thing is, could you add Malcolm to it, without changing what's already there.

27
00:01:31,803 --> 00:01:36,549
What about having him sitting on the tree trunk on the right of the picture?

28
00:01:36,549 --> 00:01:39,089
Yes, that would be fine.

29
00:01:39,089 --> 00:01:41,372
And do you want him watching the other people?

30
00:01:41,372 --> 00:01:51,764
No, he's been left out of all the fun, so I'd like him to be crying - that'll contrast nicely with the next picture, where he's laughing at the clowns in the carnival.

31
00:01:51,764 --> 00:01:53,328
Right, I'll do that.

32
00:01:53,328 --> 00:01:57,097
[滑冰插图] And then the drawing of the people ice skating in the forest,

33
00:01:57,097 --> 00:01:59,246
I wasn't too happy with that one.

34
00:01:59,246 --> 00:02:02,854
Because they've supposed to be skating on grass, aren't they?

35
00:02:02,854 --> 00:02:05,289
That's right, and it's frozen over.

36
00:02:05,289 --> 00:02:09,126
At the moment it doesn't look quite right.

37
00:02:09,126 --> 00:02:10,782
Mm, I see what you mean.

38
00:02:10,782 --> 00:02:12,546
I'll have another go at that.

39
00:02:12,546 --> 00:02:15,505
And I like the wool hats they're wearing.

40
00:02:15,505 --> 00:02:18,216
Maybe you could give each of them a scarf, as well.

41
00:02:18,216 --> 00:02:19,941
Yeah, that's easy enough.

42
00:02:19,941 --> 00:02:24,072
They can be streaming out behind the people to suggest they're skating really fast.

43
00:02:24,072 --> 00:02:25,053
Mm, great.

44
00:02:25,053 --> 00:02:28,030
Well that's all on the drawings.

45
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:39,956
[报告分工] Right. So you've finished writing your story and I just need to finish illustrating it, and my story and your drawings are done.

46
00:02:39,956 --> 00:02:49,895
(Q27（matching）) So the next thing is to decide what exactly we need to write about in the report that goes with the stories, and how we're going to divide the work.

47
00:02:49,895 --> 00:02:51,118
Right, Helen.

48
00:02:51,118 --> 00:02:56,647
What do you think about including a section on how we planned the project as a whole, Jeremy?&nbsp;

49
00:02:56,647 --> 00:02:58,393
That's probably quite important.

50
00:02:58,393 --> 00:02:59,217
Yeah.

51
00:02:59,217 --> 00:03:10,254
[Q27答案句： how do you feel ... , then 一起讨论（干扰项）， Helen： reasonable表同意➡️ Helen only] Jeremy：Well, you've had most of the good ideas so far.&nbsp; How do you feel about drafting something, then we can go through it together and discuss it?Helen： OK, that seems reasonable.

52
00:03:10,254 --> 00:03:14,981
(Q28) And I could include something on how we came up with the ideas for our two stories, couldn't I?

53
00:03:14,981 --> 00:03:23,049
[Q28答案句 ➡️ Both] Jeremy：Well I've started writing something about that, so why don't you do the same and we can include the two things.&nbsp;Helen：Right.

54
00:03:23,049 --> 00:03:26,188
(Q29) So what about our interpretation of the stories?

55
00:03:26,188 --> 00:03:32,704
Do we need to write about what we think they show, like the value of helping other people, all that sort of thing?

56
00:03:32,704 --> 00:03:35,438
That's going to come up later, isn't it?

57
00:03:35,438 --> 00:03:44,677
I think everyone in the class is going to read each other's stories and come up with their own interpretations, which we are going to discuss.

58
00:03:44,677 --> 00:03:46,463
Oh, I missed that.

59
00:03:46,463 --> 00:03:50,656
[Q29答案句： ➡️neither] H：So it isn't going to be part of the report at all?J：No.

60
00:03:50,656 --> 00:03:57,606
(Q30) But we need to write about the illustrations, because they're an essential element of children's experience of reading the stories.

61
00:03:57,606 --> 00:04:04,023
It's probably easiest for you to write that section, as you know more about drawing than I do.

62
00:04:04,023 --> 00:04:10,891
[Q30答案句： 你来做更好，另一人right 同意。➡️    Jeremy only] Helen：Maybe, but I find it quite hard to write about, I'd be happier if you did it.Jeremy：OK.

63
00:04:10,891 --> 00:04:13,391
So when do you think ...

